<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nizari Ismaili state</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Nizari Ismaili</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizari-ismailism">Nizari</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismailism">Ismaili</a> state was a network of mountain fortresses in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> governed by the Nizari branch of Ismaili <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiism">Shi'ism</a>, centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alamut">Alamut</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alborz">Alborz</a> mountains from the late eleventh century. It resisted larger powers for over a century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hulagu-khan">Hulagu</a> destroyed it in 1256 by capturing Alamut, before advancing on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> to found the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nizari-ismaili-state.jpg" alt="Nizari Ismaili state" width="320" height="275" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jolle · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alamut.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The state grew out of the split among Ismaili Shias over the succession to the Fatimid imamate, when most Ismailis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> accepted the claim of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizar-ibn-al-mustansir">Nizar ibn al-Mustansir</a> upheld by Hassan-i Sabbah. Expelled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, Hassan drew on resentment of the Sunni Seljuks, who had divided farmland into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fiefs</a> and taxed heavily. Wanted by the vizier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizam-al-mulk">Nizam al-Mulk</a>, he hid in Qazvin, sent supporters to settle in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alamut">Alamut</a> valley and entered the castle in disguise. In 1090 he took it from its Zaydi lord, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdi">Mahdi</a>, without violence; Mahdi was spared and later paid 3,000 gold dinars.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Hassan and his successors repeated this covert capture at fortresses across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, creating a state of unconnected strongholds in hostile country, with its own coinage that called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alamut">Alamut</a> the Capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daylam">Daylam</a>. In the mid-12th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizari-ismailism">Nizaris</a> acquired fortresses in the Nusayriyah Mountain Range of coastal Syria, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masyaf">Masyaf</a> and al-Kahf.</p><p>Below the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imam</a> stood a hierarchy of chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dai">da'is</a>, great da'is and ordinary da'is, with companions, sworn adherents and fida'is, mostly peasants and artisans. Each territory had a chief da'i appointed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alamut">Alamut</a> but free to act locally. Their opponents called them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/order-of-assassins">Assassins</a>, and they did kill selected leaders in public, beginning, it is widely held, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizam-al-mulk">Nizam al-Mulk</a>; yet almost any political murder of the period was attributed to them. Most of what is known about them comes from hostile sources.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1238 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizari-ismailism">Nizari</a> imam and the Abbasid caliph sent a joint embassy to the kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-ix-of-france">Louis IX of France</a> among them, seeking an alliance against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>, without result. The young imam <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rukn-al-din-khurshah">Rukn al-Din Khurshah</a> surrendered Maymun-Dizh to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hulagu-khan">Hulagu Khan</a> on 19 November 1256 after hard fighting. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongke">Möngke Khan</a> then ordered the massacre of all Nizari Ismailis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, and Rukn al-Din was killed by his Mongol guard in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>. Gerdkuh, the last stronghold in Persia, fell in 1270, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> sultan Baibars annexed the castles in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> in 1270-73. A son of Rukn al-Din recaptured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alamut">Alamut</a> in 1275, though only for a few years.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nizari Ismaili state” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Nizari Ismailis</b> who broke away after the 1094 succession crisis gave rise to the imams known today as the Aga Khans, a lineage tracing back to the Fatimid missionary network.</blockquote><a href="/empire/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Hulagu destroyed the <b>Nizari Ismaili state</b> in 1256, securing the surrender of its imam and, after the Siege of Alamut, the fortress itself.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
